Models used at runtime
----------------------
Yarvis does not include any model files. They are downloaded on first use from their
upstream sources (pinned by size and SHA-256, or by exact commit) and stored in the user's
cache folder, except Silero VAD, which comes inside the faster-whisper package:

- Kokoro-82M text-to-speech (Apache 2.0), via kokoro-onnx (MIT).
- Parakeet TDT 0.6B v2 speech recognition by NVIDIA (CC BY 4.0,
  https://huggingface.co/nvidia/parakeet-tdt-0.6b-v2), converted to MLX by the MLX
  Community (https://huggingface.co/mlx-community/parakeet-tdt-0.6b-v2), run with
  parakeet-mlx (Apache 2.0). Used on Apple Silicon Macs.
- The same Parakeet TDT 0.6B v2 model exported to ONNX by Ilya Stupakov
  (https://huggingface.co/istupakov/parakeet-tdt-0.6b-v2-onnx, CC BY 4.0), run with
  onnx-asr (MIT). Used on every other computer.
- Whisper speech recognition (MIT), via faster-whisper / CTranslate2 (MIT).
- Silero VAD (MIT).
- openWakeWord pre-trained models: melspectrogram, embedding_model and hey_jarvis, by
  David Scripka (https://github.com/dscripka/openWakeWord). These models are licensed
  under CC BY-NC-SA 4.0 and may only be used for non-commercial purposes.

Yarvis's wake-word inference (src/yarvis/speech/wakeword.py) follows the openWakeWord
reference implementation, Copyright David Scripka, licensed under the Apache License 2.0.

Services used at runtime
------------------------
- MCP servers the user configures run under their own licenses; Yarvis talks to them with
  the official MCP Python SDK (MIT), checks their arguments with jsonschema (MIT) and reads
  Agent Skills' front matter with PyYAML (MIT).
- Optional: live speech recognition by AssemblyAI (https://www.assemblyai.com), with the
  user's own API key and under AssemblyAI's terms. Its streaming API is used through
  websockets (BSD-3-Clause).
- Weather data by Open-Meteo.com (https://open-meteo.com), licensed under CC BY 4.0
  (https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/). Open-Meteo's free API is for
  non-commercial use.
